thread:
maria: Quick check — I'm planning to reduce replicas for `model-serving-sts` canary from 4 -> 2 and add a rollout annotation so we can do a partitioned drain. PR is terraform#1289. Any objections? We saw p95 tail latency spike yesterday but only during cold starts.

raj: Can you paste the TF/manifest change you're proposing? Also, do we need to touch the HPA / PDB?

maria: snippet from the TF change in the branch:```hcl
resource "kubernetes_stateful_set" "model_serving" {
  metadata { name = "model-serving-sts" }
  spec {
    replicas = 2
    update_strategy {
      type = "RollingUpdate"
      rolling_update { partition = 1 }
    }
    template {
      metadata {
        annotations = {
          "infra.redwood.io/rollout-slowdown" = "30m"
        }
      }
      spec {
        containers {
          name  = "inference"
          image = "registry/redwood/model-serving:canary"
          resources {
            requests = { cpu = "400m" memory = "1Gi" }
            limits   = { cpu = "1"   memory = "2Gi" }
          }
          lifecycle { pre_stop { exec { command = ["/bin/sleep", "8"] } } }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

sam: HPA is still enabled (minReplicas = 1, maxReplicas = 10). Lowering canary replicas is fine if we accept transient capacity loss until HPA scales. Recommend PDB minAvailable = 1 and keep preStop so in-flight requests drain. Also consider increasing pod CPU request to avoid eviction during bursts.

andrew: Small note — since this is a StatefulSet we can't rely on Deployment-style rollout annotations. The partitioned rollingUpdate is the right move. Also add an explicit annotation so our rollout controller knows to stage the change: `infra.redwood.io/staged-rollout: \"true\"` and `infra.redwood.io/rollout-window: \"15m\"`. That lets the autoscaler and the rollout watcher coordinate.

olivia: For stateful PVCs make sure disk detach times won't block deletion. If the node is slow to detach, the scale down can hang. We should set `podDisruptionBudget` and verify CSI detach timeout in the cluster. If we want zero surprises, do a manual `kubectl patch` with partitioned update during a quieter window.

infra-bot: terraform plan available: https://ci.redwood.example.com/plan/3283 — shows `module.canary.kubernetes_stateful_set.model_serving.spec.replicas: 4 -> 2` and `+ annotations.infra.redwood.io/rollout-slowdown = \"30m\"`

deploy-bot: Heads-up — canary SLO is p95 < 200ms. Dropping replicas may push p95 up until caches warm. Recommend staged steps: 4 -> 3 (30m) -> 2 (30m) and smoke check after each step. I can automate the pauses if you add `infra.redwood.io/auto-pause: \"true\"` to the manifest.

raj: LGTM on staged approach. Also, let's confirm cache priming: warm 2 pods with a synthetic load for 5 minutes after each step.

maria: I'll split TF into two commits: (1) replicas change + partitioned rollingUpdate, (2) add annotations + PDB bump. I'll add instructions in PR description for staged rollout and synthetic warmup script. Tagging @andrew and @olivia for reviews and ops checklist.

andrew: ACK — will review tomorrow morning. If you need immediate approval for the first commit to unblock infra tests, ping me.

maria: thanks everyone :thumbsup:

olivia: one more thing — can you include which nodes currently host the highest ordinals? `kubectl get pods -l app=model-serving -o wide` and add to the PR so SRE can watch disk detach behavior.

maria: good call, will include node mapping and the perf baseline. PR link once ready: https://github.com/redwood-inference/infra/pull/1289

